course: dictionary
lesson: 407
title: One word, many places
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can mean one thing in a fruit bowl and another thing in a science book. «Apple» is a noun: a round fruit that grows on a tree, red, green or yellow, with small seeds in the middle. An apple tree is the tree that grows apples, so the word can name the fruit or the tree.
explain: Context tells you which meaning you need. «Paying 1 dollar for 2 apples at 45 cents each» is about fruit you buy: the apples cost 90 cents, and 100 - 90 = 10 cents come back. Here the apples are things you count and pay for.
explain: In «Isaac Newton explained that gravity makes an apple fall», the apple is a fruit dropping from a tree. The sentence is about forces, not lunch. Ask yourself what job the word is doing, then check that your meaning fits the sentence.
explain: Phrases and stories can stretch a word. «An apple a day keeps the doctor away» is a saying about eating well, not a promise. In a design story, «the apple bite is a computer joke» is not about eating at all, so the words around it are your clue.
fact: An apple is a round fruit with small seeds in the middle.
fact: An apple tree is the tree that grows apples, and «apples» is the plural of apple.
fact: Context clues show which meaning of a word the writer is using.
fact: «The cashew nut grows under a fleshy cashew apple», so the word can also appear in the name of another fruit.
fact: You can always check a meaning by putting it into the sentence and asking whether it still makes sense.
exercise: «Paying 1 dollar for 2 apples at 45 cents each» : how many cents come back? | 90 cents | 1 dollar | *10 cents | 55 cents
exercise: What is an apple tree? | A round red fruit with seeds | *The tree that grows apples | A kind of table | A pie with a crust
exercise: In «Isaac Newton explained that gravity makes an apple fall», what is the apple? | *A fruit falling from a tree | A bag of two apples | A computer | A bell in a classroom
test: Why is it worth checking a design story? | Because apples are always red | Because designers never speak | Because all design stories are true | *Because some stories grow bigger than the truth
test: What colours can an apple be, according to its meaning? | Blue, purple or black | *Red, green or yellow | Only red | Brown and sticky
exam: In the saying «an apple a day keeps the doctor away», what does apple mean? | *A round fruit, eaten as part of healthy eating | A bell in the school hall | A computer | A teacher's desk
